// Drums: a drum machine's voices, each made as its circuit makes it.
//
// A drum is a few numbers (`Drum`) and six parts, any of them silent:
// - a tone: a resonator a trigger's pulse sets ringing, which starts high and settles as the
//   pulse dies (a bass drum's boom, a snare's shell), and a second tone beside it. A harder
//   blow stretches a skin, so it sounds a little higher; and a skin struck again while it
//   still rings goes on from where it is, so a roll isn't one hit over and over;
// - a thump: the trigger's pulse itself, a half sine a millisecond or two long, which is what
//   a bass drum's attack is (not a click of noise);
// - noise through a band, above a low cut and under a top: a snare's wires, and a clap's hands,
//   which are that noise in a few quick bursts and then a tail. A snare's wires lie on its
//   skin, so they rattle with it: their noise swells and thins with the tone's own wave;
// - metal: six square waves at ratios no chord has, through the same band and cut: a drum
//   machine's hi-hat;
// - a plate: a real cymbal's bronze, as its modes: a hundred and more of them, each at its own
//   pitch and dying at its own rate, the high ones soonest; all struck at once and then
//   trading what they have, a wash of noise with partials in it (a crash), or two of them
//   standing out as a note (a ride);
// - a head: an acoustic drum's skin as its own modes, struck at a place by a stick that is on
//   it a millisecond, its pitch a little high at the blow and settling, with a shell's modes
//   beside it; and wires lying on its other head, which throws them off and catches them
//   again: each landing rings that head and the shell, and takes speed from the struck one,
//   so the buzz is the drum's own, comes in bursts as the head moves and dies with it;
// - a click: a stick's tip, a few milliseconds of noise.
//
// The parts and their tunings follow the Roland TR-808's circuits: its bass drum is a damped
// resonator pinged by a pulse, whose pitch sags as the pulse ends (Werner, Abel and Smith, "A
// physically-informed, circuit-bendable, digital model of the Roland TR-808 bass drum circuit",
// DAFx-14, 2014), and its cymbals are six free-running square waves through two band-passes
// (Werner, Abel and Smith, "The TR-808 cymbal: a physically-informed, circuit-bendable, digital
// model", ICMC 2014). A snare's two tones are two of a skin's modes, 1 and 2.14 times its lowest
// (an ideal membrane's are at 1, 1.59, 2.14, 2.30...: Fletcher and Rossing, The Physics of
// Musical Instruments, 2nd ed., 1998, ch. 3).
//
// The plate follows what a struck cymbal is (Fletcher and Rossing, ch. 20: hundreds of modes,
// nearer each other in pitch than the ear tells apart, and energy passing from the low ones to
// the high ones in the first tenths of a second after a hard blow) and what two anechoic
// recordings show (the University of Iowa Musical Instrument Samples' 18-inch crash and 21-inch
// ride: docs/citations.md): a partial every 30 to 50 Hz; a crash loudest round 4 kHz and, as a
// whole, in its first 20 ms, however it is struck (struck hard on its shoulder its 2, 4 and
// 8 kHz octaves rise 7 to 12 dB more, 120 to 280 ms after the stick; struck less hard, or on
// its bow, none of them rises 2 dB), each octave up ringing a little shorter (5 s at 500 Hz,
// 3 s at 8 kHz), and the harder struck the nearer to noise between its partials; a ride level
// from 500 Hz to 8 kHz at the stick and its top gone in a second while its low modes ring for
// ten. Six squares held for two seconds are six pitches, a bell: in the 4 kHz octave the
// squares' crash has 4 partials standing out and the real one 52. No partial of either cymbal
// sags in pitch after the blow, so none does here.
//
// The squares and the noise run on whether or not the drum sounds, as the machine's do, so each
// hit catches them somewhere else and no two hits are the same samples. Everything is f32
// arithmetic in a fixed order, and the noise is seeded, so both hosts give the same samples.
//
// The machine's patches are fitted to no recording: an ear's notes on the first set (a snare
// that was "a sine and a spray of noise", a clap of three identical bursts, a bass drum whose
// attack was a tick) are what they answer. The cymbals follow two anechoic ones, and the
// snare drum (`SNARE_DRUM`) one recorded close and dry, wires off and on: docs/citations.md.

use std::audio::SAMPLE_RATE
use std::math::{PI, TAU}
use std::collections::Vec
use tone::{Band, Mode, Noise, Svf, band, decay, gain_of, one_pole, pulse, seeded, warm}

/// The audio thread's rate.
const RATE: f32 = f32(SAMPLE_RATE)

/// The squares' frequencies (Hz): those commonly given for the six oscillators of the TR-808's
/// cymbal and hi-hat circuit. They aren't checked against a machine.
const METAL_HZ: [f32; 6] = [205.3, 304.4, 369.6, 522.7, 540.0, 800.0]

/// The plate's modes: one every `PLATE_GAP` Hz, or every `PLATE_SHARE`th of the pitch where
/// that is more (over 2 kHz, where the ear tells fewer apart); and the most of them. Under
/// 1 kHz, where they are few, each is up to four tenths of that off its place. Over 1 kHz
/// that is only the gap on average: the gap from each mode to the next is any size, as a
/// plate's are, so some lie close enough to beat slowly. (Each near its place, none did, and
/// every third of an octave of a cymbal fell as smoothly as one tone with an envelope: from
/// one twentieth of a second to the next the crash's 4 and 8 kHz thirds moved 0.3 dB, where
/// the anechoic crash's move 0.9 and 0.6 to 0.7, as bands of noise do. That was heard as an
/// electronic edge.)
const PLATE_GAP: f32 = 40.0
const PLATE_SHARE: f32 = 50.0
const PLATE_MOST: u32 = 256
/// How hard the plate's bending shakes its modes: a third of an octave is pushed by the square
/// of what bends the plate under it (beside the plate's level; times the patch's `wash`). Set
/// by the anechoic crash's 500 Hz octave at its hardest blow, which falls 9 dB in its first
/// 20 ms and 19 dB in its first 150 ms, then 5 dB in the next 450: what a low mode gives away
/// goes by the square of what it has.
const CASCADE: f32 = 0.28
/// How much less a third's sound bends the plate for each octave up it is (the power of two it
/// falls by). A plate is heard by how fast it moves and bent by how far, so the knock and the
/// low modes bend it far more than modes as loud at 4 kHz, and it is they that feed the rest.
const CASCADE_FALLS: f32 = 2.0
/// How fast a blow sets a plate's modes trading (a second, at the loudness of the hardest
/// blow; it goes by how hard the plate swings, the root of how loud it is), the seconds the
/// trading takes to fall away to 1/e once nothing sets it, and how fast each mode trades with
/// the next when all are: the share of its energy a second that changes hands, for each Hz
/// of the usual gap between them. A plate has a mode every few Hz and a blow on its edge
/// sets them all going, a hard one little more than a middling one: the anechoic crash
/// struck hard on its shoulder is 0.12 of the way from tones to noise in its 2 and 4 kHz
/// octaves at 0.3 s and 0.07 at 1.5 s; struck 15 dB less hard there, 0.08 and 0.04 (and
/// struck lightly on its bow, 0.01). This plate's modes are 40 Hz apart and more, so each
/// stands for the crowd between it and the next.
const CROWD_RATE: f32 = 72.0
const CROWD_STAYS: f32 = 0.5
const CHURN: f32 = 0.6
/// The modes of a stick's tick.
const PLATE_TICKS: u32 = 24

/// What makes a drum's sound. A level of 0 leaves a part out.
pub struct Drum: Copy {
    /// The tone: where it settles (Hz), its level, its seconds to fall 60 dB, how many times
    /// its pitch it starts above it (1: an octave above), and the seconds that takes to come
    /// most of the way down.
    pub tone_hz: f32 = 55.0,
    pub tone: f32 = 0.0,
    pub tone_t60: f32 = 0.5,
    pub sweep: f32 = 0.0,
    pub sweep_time: f32 = 0.02,
    /// How much lower than the hardest blow's the softest blow's tones are (cents): a hard
    /// blow stretches the skin.
    pub tension: f32 = 0.0,
    /// How unlike a roll's hits are (0: alike). A hit within 150 ms of the one before is part
    /// of a roll, and is its own: at 1 its level is drawn within 1 dB either way, its pitch
    /// within 10 cents, its brightness within 0.4 of an octave of its noise's corner, and the
    /// stick lands nearer the head's centre or its edge (more of the first tone or of the
    /// second), with a small lean for the weaker hand (half a decibel, 3 cents). The draws are
    /// seeded, so a take repeats. A hit by itself, and a roll's first, take no draw: they are
    /// as they always were. (Every second hit softer, lower and duller by a fixed amount was
    /// heard as a machine taking turns.)
    pub hands: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The head: its level; its main mode (Hz); the seconds that mode takes to fall 60 dB
    /// with the wires off (the others by `HEAD_RINGS`); where the stick lands, from 0 (the
    /// centre) to 1 (the edge), and how far that moves from one hit to the next; the cents the
    /// head is above its pitch at the hardest blow and the seconds it takes to settle most of
    /// the way; and the stick's contact at the softest blow (ms: the hardest is half as long,
    /// so it rings the higher modes harder). The head's modes, their rings and how hard a
    /// blow at the centre and off it rings each are a recorded snare drum's (`HEAD_RATIOS`):
    /// a blow at the centre rings the first and the fourth, and a blow nearer the edge more
    /// of the others. Above those six the head has hundreds more, which a few stand for
    /// (`HEAD_MORE`): the stick's crack is the head's own. Every hit lands at a place and a
    /// weight of its own (seeded draws), a lone backbeat too. (That drum's main mode is 26 to
    /// 45 cents high at a hard blow and settled 80 ms on; its top with the wires off falls
    /// 9 dB an octave from 1.6 to 4 kHz and 24 dB from 1.6 to 8 kHz, as a stick's push a
    /// quarter of a millisecond long gives.)
    pub head: f32 = 0.0,
    pub head_hz: f32 = 190.0,
    pub head_t60: f32 = 0.25,
    pub spot: f32 = 0.25,
    pub spot_sway: f32 = 0.12,
    pub stretch: f32 = 35.0,
    pub stretch_time: f32 = 0.04,
    pub contact_ms: f32 = 1.2,
    /// The drum's other modes (`SHELL_RATIOS`: three that ring longest, and the snare-side
    /// head's own): their level beside the head's.
    pub shell: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The wires: their level, and how hard they are held against the head (in the hardest
    /// blow's swing). They lie on the snare-side head, which moves with the struck one, and
    /// are thrown off as it swings back faster than they can follow; each time it catches a
    /// strand again the strand slaps it and bounces, and each slap rings that head's modes:
    /// the buzz is the drum ringing, in the drum's own colour, and there is no noise in it
    /// but what the strands' landings make. The head gives each strand it catches some of its
    /// speed, and the strands lying on it damp it, so a drum with its wires on rings shorter.
    /// (Heard as a band of noise let through after each slap, the buzz had the right texture
    /// and did not sit with the drum: a tone and a hiss with nothing between. Built by
    /// reasoning as one drum, with a buzz level from 500 Hz to 8 kHz and 6 to 9 dB under the
    /// tone, it was heard as worse: a recorded drum's buzz lies from 2 to 10 kHz, 18 dB under
    /// its tone at first and as loud as it once the tone has fallen, and its middle is its
    /// own modes, not its wires. See `WIRES`.) With wires, a hit has no noise of its own.
    pub wires: f32 = 0.0,
    pub wires_hold: f32 = 0.05,
    /// A second tone: its pitch, its level and its seconds to fall 60 dB. It sweeps as the
    /// first does.
    pub second_hz: f32 = 330.0,
    pub second: f32 = 0.0,
    pub second_t60: f32 = 0.1,
    /// How hard the tones are driven into soft clipping (0: not at all): a boom a small
    /// speaker can play.
    pub drive: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The thump: the trigger's pulse, its level and its length (ms).
    pub thump: f32 = 0.0,
    pub thump_ms: f32 = 1.5,
    /// The noise: its level, its band's centre (Hz) and sharpness, the corners below and above
    /// which it is cut (Hz), and its seconds to fall 60 dB.
    pub noise: f32 = 0.0,
    pub band_hz: f32 = 5000.0,
    pub band_q: f32 = 0.7,
    pub cut_hz: f32 = 200.0,
    pub top_hz: f32 = 20000.0,
    pub noise_t60: f32 = 0.2,
    /// How far the noise rattles with the tones' wave: 0 steady, 1 swelling to twice and
    /// thinning to nothing with each turn of it.
    pub rattle: f32 = 0.0,
    /// A clap's hands: how many bursts come before the tail, the seconds between them, how far
    /// that varies (a share of it, either way), each burst's seconds to fall 60 dB, and each
    /// burst's level beside the one before.
    pub bursts: u32 = 0,
    pub burst_gap: f32 = 0.011,
    pub burst_sway: f32 = 0.0,
    pub burst_t60: f32 = 0.03,
    pub burst_fall: f32 = 1.0,
    /// The metal's level, through the noise's band and cut, and how much higher than the
    /// machine's the squares are tuned (1: as they were).
    pub metal: f32 = 0.0,
    pub metal_tune: f32 = 1.0,
    /// The plate: its level; its lowest and highest modes (Hz); where its modes are loudest
    /// (Hz), and the decibels an octave they fall off below that and above it; the seconds a
    /// mode at 1 kHz or under takes to fall 60 dB, how much shorter each octave over 1 kHz
    /// rings (the power of the pitch: 0.55 is the anechoic crash, 0.86 the ride, whose top is
    /// gone while its low modes wash on), and the decibels a second every mode dies faster
    /// by than that; and how much duller the softest blow's plate is (decibels an octave,
    /// over 1 kHz). A cymbal in a beat is shorter than one alone: with every mode faster by
    /// the same amount, the balance between its octaves as it dies is the longer cymbal's,
    /// only sooner. (A ride shortened by a steeper power, and with its modes under 1 kHz
    /// ringing on past the one at 1 kHz, had its low modes last seven to eleven seconds over
    /// a top gone in one: at 1.5 s its 500 Hz octave was 32 dB over its 4 to 8 kHz ones,
    /// where the anechoic ride's is 14 dB over: a gong.)
    pub plate: f32 = 0.0,
    pub plate_low: f32 = 300.0,
    pub plate_high: f32 = 14000.0,
    pub plate_hz: f32 = 4000.0,
    pub plate_under: f32 = 6.0,
    pub plate_over: f32 = 10.0,
    pub plate_t60: f32 = 3.0,
    pub plate_damp: f32 = 0.3,
    pub plate_fade: f32 = 0.0,
    pub plate_touch: f32 = 4.0,
    /// The wash (0: none; 1: all of this). The stick strikes the modes over 2 kHz every one
    /// alike, as a short blow does, and together as loud as one tone at the plate's level,
    /// not by the plate's shape (from 1 to 2 kHz, part each way); and after the blow the
    /// plate's bending passes up what the stick's knock and the low modes have: each third of
    /// an octave of modes is pushed, by the plate's shape, by the square of what sounds two
    /// thirds or more under it (the knock most of all), and what it is given, those lose. A
    /// plate struck hard passes energy up from the low modes first set going to its high ones
    /// (Legge and Fletcher, "Nonlinearity, chaos, and the sound of shallow gongs", J. Acoust.
    /// Soc. Am. 86(6), 1989), its modes exchanging energy until their motion is chaotic
    /// (Chaigne, Touzé and Thomas, "Nonlinear vibrations and chaos in gongs and cymbals",
    /// Acoust. Sci. & Tech. 26(5), 2005). So what blooms after the stick comes from
    /// the plate's own motion, can't be more than the knock and the low modes had, and is
    /// less for a soft blow. (Fed white noise for a fixed time, the bloom was the same
    /// whatever was struck; fed by the octave under each third with nothing lost, the 8 kHz
    /// octave swelled 19 dB where the anechoic crash's swells 7.)
    pub wash: f32 = 0.0,
    /// How far each mode's strength wanders (0: not at all; 1: about 8 dB either way, over a
    /// few tenths of a second, with no period): in a plate rung hard each partial comes and
    /// goes as the modes trade energy. With every mode steady, a third of an octave of a crash
    /// had the same spectrum from one sixth of a second to the next (0.99 alike), a frozen
    /// hiss; with each mode given a twin to beat with, a field of tremolos, which nothing
    /// struck does. The anechoic crash's is 0.63 to 0.70 alike, and the ride's 0.95. (Every
    /// strength starts at 1 and they spread from there, so a plate with much of this grows
    /// louder over its first quarter of a second: a plate whose modes trade, by `crowd`,
    /// needs none.)
    pub restless: f32 = 0.0,
    /// How much of a noise a blow makes of the plate (0: its modes ring as tones; 1: once the
    /// blow has set them trading, a light blow less and for less long, each mode and the
    /// next trade swing at random, the two keeping what they had together, so each is a band
    /// of noise, and energy spreads up and down the plate without any being made; a little
    /// of it, and they are tones whose strengths move as a ride's do). A ping's two modes and
    /// a tick's stand apart and don't trade.
    pub crowd: f32 = 0.0,
    /// A plate's knock, the stick's low body: its level beside the plate's, its pitch (Hz),
    /// how far either side of that it reaches (octaves), and the decibels more than the rest
    /// of the hit it falls for the softest blow (a cymbal's low body is rung by a hard blow
    /// far more than by a light one). The plate's modes within that reach of the pitch are
    /// struck that much harder (most at the pitch), together as loud as one tone at this
    /// level: the low body is a handful of the plate's own modes, which
    /// trade and pass their energy up the plate as any do. (As one tone of its own at 410 Hz
    /// it stood 42 dB over the middle of its octave, where the anechoic crash's strongest low
    /// partial, at 407 to 410 Hz, stands 17 to 22 dB over, in an octave 0.07 to 0.25 of the
    /// way to noise: a bell, or a gong.)
    pub knock: f32 = 0.0,
    pub knock_hz: f32 = 410.0,
    pub knock_wide: f32 = 0.5,
    pub knock_touch: f32 = 0.0,
    /// A ping: two modes that stand out of the plate, at this pitch and half as high again
    /// (the 21-inch ride's strongest above its wash are at 2484 and 3721 Hz), their level
    /// beside the whole plate's and their seconds to fall 60 dB: a ride's note.
    pub ping: f32 = 0.0,
    pub ping_hz: f32 = 2500.0,
    pub ping_t60: f32 = 1.2,
    /// The stick's tick: modes round `tick_hz` (from six tenths of it to half as high again)
    /// that the blow rings as hard as this beside the whole plate and that are gone in
    /// `tick_t60` seconds. (Noise in that band for as long was heard as static at every
    /// stroke; the anechoic ride's 8 kHz band is steady partials from the stick on.)
    pub tick: f32 = 0.0,
    pub tick_hz: f32 = 11000.0,
    pub tick_t60: f32 = 0.08,
    /// The click's level.
    pub click: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The decibels between the softest hit and the hardest; how much duller the softest
    /// hit's noise and metal are (the corner, Hz, they're cut above at a velocity of 0; at 1,
    /// nothing is cut); and how much shorter its noise rings (0: as long; 0.5: half as long,
    /// and the hardest half as long again).
    pub range_db: f32 = 26.0,
    pub soft_hz: f32 = 5000.0,
    pub ring_touch: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The level of the whole drum.
    pub level: f32 = 1.0,
}

/// One hit ringing.
struct Hit: Copy {
    on: bool = false,
    /// Its level, as its velocity set it.
    level: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The tones' phases (0 to 1), their levels and decays, and how far above their pitch they
    /// still are and that's decay.
    phase: f32 = 0.0,
    phase2: f32 = 0.0,
    /// How many times the patch's pitch its tones are (a softer blow's are lower).
    tuned: f32 = 1.0,
    tone: f32 = 0.0,
    tone_fall: f32 = 0.0,
    second: f32 = 0.0,
    second_fall: f32 = 0.0,
    sweep: f32 = 0.0,
    sweep_fall: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The thump: its samples in all, and those still to come.
    thump_all: u32 = 0,
    thump_left: u32 = 0,
    /// The noise's and the metal's level and decay; the bursts still to come, the samples to
    /// the next, the level the next starts at, and the tail's decay.
    hiss: f32 = 0.0,
    hiss_fall: f32 = 0.0,
    bursts: u32 = 0,
    burst_in: u32 = 0,
    burst_level: f32 = 0.0,
    tail_fall: f32 = 0.0,
    click: f32 = 0.0,
    /// Its share of full while it's choked: 1 until then.
    fade: f32 = 1.0,
    fade_fall: f32 = 1.0,
}

/// A drum being played: what the audio thread owns of it.
pub struct Drummer {
    patch: Drum,
    /// Two hits ring at once: a hit struck over another lets the first die away fast.
    hits: [Hit; 2],
    next: u32,
    noise: Noise,
    metal: [f32; 6],
    band: Band,
    band_svf: Svf,
    cut: Band,
    cut_svf: Svf,
    top: Band,
    top_svf: Svf,
    /// The dulling of soft hits: a one-pole's share and its state.
    soft: f32,
    soft_held: f32,
    click_held: f32,
    /// The plate: its modes (the ping's two first, then from the lowest up), each one's pitch
    /// (Hz), its level in the plate, and the octaves it is over 1 kHz (0 under it); how many
    /// of them still ring (from the first); the wash now feeding them, and the share of it
    /// each sample keeps; and whether a hand has stopped them (their decays are the choke's).
    plate: Vec<Mode>,
    plate_hz: Vec<f32>,
    plate_level: Vec<f32>,
    plate_over: Vec<f32>,
    /// Each mode's seconds to fall 60 dB, and the share of a blow it gets by the wash.
    plate_rings: Vec<f32>,
    plate_washed: Vec<f32>,
    ringing: u32,
    stopped: bool,
    /// The drum's modes: the head's six, the stand-ins for its higher ones, the shell's three
    /// and the snare-side head's; each one's pitch (Hz, the head at rest) and seconds to fall
    /// 60 dB; what the blow now landing gives each (it comes in over the stick's contact, of
    /// `contact` samples, `contact_left` to come); the cents the head is above its pitch now
    /// and the share of them it keeps each 16 samples; the samples to the next setting of its
    /// pitch; whether any of it sounds; the level its swing is measured against; and what a
    /// change in each of the six's speed is as a strike (one over the sine of its turn a
    /// sample).
    skin: Vec<Mode>,
    skin_hz: Vec<f32>,
    skin_t60: Vec<f32>,
    skin_blow: Vec<f32>,
    contact: u32,
    contact_left: u32,
    stretched: f32,
    stretch_keeps: f32,
    skin_in: u32,
    skin_live: bool,
    skin_ref: f32,
    skin_slow: [f32; 6],
    /// The share of a slap each of the snare-side head's modes takes (their squares sum to 1).
    slap_share: Vec<f32>,
    /// The wires: where each strand is and how fast it moves (in the hardest blow's swing, a
    /// sample), where the head was a sample ago, and whether each is off the head.
    wire: [f32; 6],
    wire_v: [f32; 6],
    head_was: f32,
    apart: [bool; 6],
    /// The samples since the last hit (as `sound` counts them), whether that hit was the
    /// weaker hand's, and what a roll's hits are drawn from.
    since: u32,
    weak: bool,
    touch: Noise,
    /// Each of the plate's modes' strength now (it wanders about 1), the samples to its next
    /// move, and the level the plate's modes are measured against (the patch's, whatever the
    /// blow).
    plate_now: Vec<f32>,
    wander_in: u32,
    plate_ref: f32,
    /// Where each mode's strength has wandered (the log of it), which third of an octave
    /// above the plate's lowest mode each mode is in, and what each third sounded at the last
    /// sample (beside the plate's level).
    plate_drift: Vec<f32>,
    plate_third: Vec<u32>,
    thirds: [f32; 20],
    /// What the stick's own blow gives each washed mode alike, and the share of the knock
    /// each mode takes (their squares sum to 1).
    plate_even: f32,
    plate_knock: Vec<f32>,
    /// How far the plate's modes have been set trading (0 to 1); the samples to the next
    /// round of trades and whether it starts from the first mode or the second; the first of
    /// the modes that trade and the one after the last; and the angle each mode and the next
    /// are turned through at most in a round when all are trading.
    crowd: f32,
    trade_in: u32,
    trade_from: u32,
    trade_low: u32,
    trade_high: u32,
    plate_turn: Vec<f32>,
    /// The highest third of an octave that has modes of the plate (the tick's aside); how
    /// much each third's modes take of a drive (the squares of their shares, summed); and how
    /// much each third has now (its modes' swings squared, over the last millisecond or so).
    top_third: u32,
    third_takes: [f32; 20],
    third_has: [f32; 20],
}

/// The struck head's six lowest modes beside its main one, how hard a blow rings each beside
/// the first (by where it lands: `spot_rings`), and their seconds to fall 60 dB beside the
/// main one's, wires off. Measured from a snare drum recorded close and dry, wires off and on
/// (Virtuosity Drums' snare microphone: docs/citations.md): struck at its centre with its
/// wires off, its partials are at 226 Hz (the main one), 336 and 488 Hz (6 and 15 dB under it
/// over its first third of a second), and 181, 629 and 671 Hz (21 to 22 dB under); off its
/// centre the second and third are as strong as the first and the 181 Hz one is gone. They
/// fall 30 dB in 150, 320, 250, 150 and 130 ms (the 629 Hz one's fall is not clear of its
/// neighbours'). These are not an ideal membrane's ratios (1, 1.59, 2.14, 2.30): a drum's two
/// heads and the air between them move as one.
const HEAD_RATIOS: [f32; 6] = [1.0, 1.49, 2.16, 0.80, 2.78, 2.97]
const HEAD_GAINS: [f32; 6] = [1.0, 0.34, 0.52, 0.11, 1.1, 0.12]
const HEAD_RINGS: [f32; 6] = [1.0, 2.13, 1.67, 1.0, 1.33, 0.87]
/// A head has far more modes than those six. `HEAD_MORE` stand for those from 6.2 to 42 times
/// the main one (1.4 to 9.5 kHz for that drum), a fixed ratio apart and each up to four
/// tenths of the gap off its place, each rung this hard beside the first: the crack of the
/// stick is the head's own, and is duller for a softer blow because the stick is on the head
/// longer. They ring 0.83 of the main mode's time at 9 times its pitch, less as the pitch
/// rises, down to 0.45 of it (wires off, that drum's 2, 4 and 8 kHz octaves fall as 0.25,
/// 0.17 and 0.15 s to 60 dB; its octaves from 1 to 8 kHz are 15, 20, 25 and 33 dB under its
/// 250 Hz one from 10 to 30 ms, and this drum's are within 3 dB of that).
const HEAD_MORE: u32 = 24
const HEAD_MORE_FROM: f32 = 6.2
const HEAD_MORE_TO: f32 = 42.0
const HEAD_MORE_GAIN: f32 = 0.055
/// The drum's other modes: three that ring longest (that drum's at 839, 1190 and 1341 Hz,
/// which fall 30 dB in 390, 210 and 280 ms) and the snare-side head's own (552 Hz, which the
/// wires ring and damp): each one's pitch beside the main mode's, how hard a blow rings it
/// beside the main one, and its seconds to fall 60 dB with the wires off.
const SHELL: u32 = 4
const SHELL_RATIOS: [f32; 4] = [3.71, 5.27, 5.93, 2.44]
const SHELL_GAINS: [f32; 4] = [0.062, 0.062, 0.028, 0.03]
const SHELL_RINGS: [f32; 4] = [0.78, 0.42, 0.56, 0.16]
/// The snare-side head, which the wires lie on. `SNARE_MODES` stand for its modes from 2.3 to
/// 57.5 times the struck head's main one (520 Hz to 13 kHz for that drum; its wires add most
/// from 3 to 8 kHz, level by thirds of an octave, 4 to 6 dB less at 2 and 10 kHz, 13 dB less
/// at 12.5 kHz), a fixed ratio apart (each up to four tenths of the gap off its place), each
/// damped by the wires until it is as wide as the gap to the next (it rings 60 ms at 520 Hz,
/// 16 ms at 2 kHz, 4 ms at 8 kHz): what a slap rings is a short burst in the drum's own
/// colour, which moves with the drum's tuning. Under 2 kHz what the buzz adds is the floor
/// the drum's own partials stand in: with no buzz under 1.8 kHz this drum's 838 and 1190 Hz
/// partials stood 20 to 27 dB over the middle of their thirds of an octave from 30 to 150 ms
/// where that drum's stand 9 to 15, and it was heard as a tin can. And how much of a blow
/// reaches that head at once, through the air in the drum (little: that drum's top in its
/// first 10 ms is its struck head's).
const SNARE_MODES: u32 = 48
const SNARE_FROM: f32 = 2.3
const SNARE_TO: f32 = 57.5
const SNARE_AIR: f32 = 0.01
/// The wires: `WIRES` strands, each a mass on a spring (the middle one would swing at 300 Hz,
/// a fifth damped; the others are pulled from 0.6 to 1.4 times as hard and held from 0.7 to
/// 1.3 times as hard, so they leave and land at different times), which bounce: a strand the
/// head catches leaves it again with this share of the speed it landed with, until it lands
/// too slowly to (under `WIRE_STILL` of the hardest blow's swing a sample). So the buzz
/// outlasts the blow's first swings and has no beat of the head's in it, as that drum's
/// (its 4 kHz octave is 18 dB under its 250 Hz one from 10 to 30 ms and as loud as it from
/// 30 to 100 ms; its buzz reaches half its loudest within 3 ms of the blow).
///
/// Then: how hard a slap rings the snare-side head, and the power of its speed that goes by
/// (under 1: a strand landing ten times as fast rings the head three times as hard; that
/// drum's buzz is 18, 16 and 14 dB under its tone for blows 9 dB apart); what a slap rings
/// of the struck head's third and fourth modes and of the snare-side head's own beside that
/// (with its wires on that drum's 181, 487 and 552 Hz partials stand as high as its main
/// one; most goes to the 181 Hz one, which keeps a body under the middle as the drum rings
/// on: from 100 to 300 ms that drum's 150 to 300 Hz is 2 dB under its 400 Hz to 1.5 kHz);
/// and the tilt that keeps the buzz level across its octaves while it lasts.
///
/// And what the wires cost the head: the share of its speed all the strands together take
/// from its main mode when it catches them, how much of that its second mode pays, and the
/// seconds the head would take to fall 60 dB by the damping of all of them lying on it alone
/// (that drum's 250 Hz octave falls 30 dB in 130 ms with its wires off and 60 ms with them
/// on). The wires are moved by the head's first two modes and, a little (`WIRE_LONG`), by
/// the drum's three long-ringing ones, so the buzz lasts under them as they ring; never by a
/// mode their slaps ring, which would feed itself.
const WIRES: u32 = 6
const WIRE_PULL: f32 = 0.00154
const WIRE_DRAG: f32 = 0.0157
const WIRE_BOUNCE: f32 = 0.9
const WIRE_STILL: f32 = 0.00001
const SLAP_GAIN: f32 = 1.27
const SLAP_CURVE: f32 = 0.5
const SLAP_LOWS: [f32; 3] = [0.03, 0.2, 0.3]
const SLAP_TILT: f32 = 0.3
const WIRE_LOAD: f32 = 0.11
const WIRE_SHARES: [f32; 2] = [1.0, 0.3]
const WIRE_REST: f32 = 1.0
const WIRE_LONG: f32 = 0.1

/// How much of each of the head's modes a blow `r` of the way from the centre to the edge
/// rings: the mode's shape there, roughly (the first and fourth are whole at the centre; the
/// rest are nothing there).
fn spot_rings(r: f32) -> [f32; 6] {
    let across = sin(PI * r)
    [
        cos(0.5 * PI * r),
        across,
        across * across,
        (1.0 - r * r) * cos(3.6 * r),
        across * across * across,
        sin(TAU * r) * (1.0 - 0.4 * r),
    ]
}

/// The seconds a plate's mode at `hz` takes to fall 60 dB.
fn plate_ring(p: Drum, hz: f32) -> f32 {
    // (Over 8 kHz a mode rings shorter again by its pitch: the anechoic crash's 16 kHz octave
    // rings 0.9 to 1.5 s where its 8 kHz one rings 2.3 to 3.1 s, and beside the 4 to 8 kHz
    // octaves its 10 to 20 kHz falls 10 dB in its first 0.3 s.)
    let middle = p.plate_t60 * pow(max(hz, 1000.0) / 1000.0, -p.plate_damp)
    let alone = middle * 8000.0 / max(hz, 8000.0)
    1.0 / (1.0 / alone + p.plate_fade / 60.0)
}

/// A drummer for `patch`. `seed` sets its noise.
pub fn drummer(patch: Drum, seed: u32 = 1) -> Drummer {
    var metal = [0.0; 6]
    for i in 0..6 {
        // Each square starts somewhere of its own.
        metal[i] = fract(0.137 * f32(i + 1) + 0.61 * f32(seed % 7))
    }
    // The plate's modes, if it has a plate: the ping's two, then one for each place from the
    // lowest up, a little off it, at a level of its own under the plate's shape. The levels
    // are set so the modes together are as loud as one tone at the plate's level.
    var plate: Vec<Mode> = Vec::new()
    var plate_hz: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var plate_level: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var plate_over: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var plate_rings: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var plate_washed: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var plate_even = 0.0
    var trade_low = 0
    var trade_high = 0
    var plate_knock: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    if patch.plate > 0.0 {
        if patch.ping > 0.0 {
            for k in 0..2 {
                let hz = patch.ping_hz * (1.0 + 0.5 * f32(k))
                var m = Mode {}
                m.tune(hz, patch.ping_t60)
                plate.push(m)
                plate_hz.push(hz)
                plate_level.push(patch.ping * (1.0 - 0.3 * f32(k)))
                plate_over.push(0.0)
                plate_rings.push(patch.ping_t60)
                plate_washed.push(0.0)
            }
        }
        var grain = seeded(seed + 77)
        var place = patch.plate_low
        var squares = 0.0
        let first = plate.len()
        while place < patch.plate_high && plate.len() + PLATE_TICKS + 2 <= PLATE_MOST {
            let gap = max(PLATE_GAP, place / PLATE_SHARE)
            // (Under 1 kHz, where the modes are few, each is near its place; over it, the
            // gap from each to the next is any size, this on average.)
            let evenly = place < 1000.0
            let hz = if evenly { place + 0.4 * gap * grain.next() } else { place }
            let octaves = log2(hz / patch.plate_hz)
            let shape = if octaves < 0.0 { patch.plate_under * octaves } else {
                -patch.plate_over * octaves
            }
            let level = gain_of(shape) * (0.4 + 0.6 * abs(grain.next()))
            let ring = plate_ring(patch, hz)
            let over = max(log2(hz / 1000.0), 0.0)
            var m = Mode {}
            m.tune(hz, ring)
            plate.push(m)
            plate_hz.push(hz)
            plate_level.push(level)
            plate_over.push(over)
            plate_rings.push(ring)
            plate_washed.push(min(over, 1.0))
            squares += level * level
            place += if evenly { gap } else { gap * -log(max(0.5 + 0.5 * grain.next(), 1e-4)) }
        }
        for i in first..plate.len() {
            plate_level[i] = plate_level[i] / sqrt(max(squares, 1e-12))
        }
        plate_even = 1.0 / sqrt(f32(max(plate.len() - first, 1)))
        trade_low = first
        trade_high = plate.len()
        // The knock's share of each mode: most at its pitch, none its width off.
        var struck = 0.0
        let wide = max(patch.knock_wide, 0.05)
        for i in 0..plate.len() {
            let off = if i >= first { abs(log2(plate_hz[i] / patch.knock_hz)) / wide } else { 1.0 }
            let share = if patch.knock > 0.0 && off < 1.0 { 0.5 + 0.5 * cos(PI * off) } else { 0.0 }
            plate_knock.push(share)
            struck += share * share
        }
        for i in 0..plate.len() {
            plate_knock[i] = plate_knock[i] / sqrt(max(struck, 1e-12))
        }
        // The tick's modes, last: they die first.
        if patch.tick > 0.0 {
            for k in 0..PLATE_TICKS {
                let along = (f32(k) + 0.4 * grain.next()) / f32(PLATE_TICKS)
                let hz = min(patch.tick_hz * exp2(1.27 * along - 0.74), 0.45 * RATE)
                var m = Mode {}
                m.tune(hz, patch.tick_t60)
                plate.push(m)
                plate_hz.push(hz)
                plate_level.push(
                    patch.tick * (0.5 + 0.5 * abs(grain.next())) / sqrt(f32(PLATE_TICKS)),
                )
                plate_over.push(max(log2(hz / 1000.0), 0.0))
                plate_rings.push(patch.tick_t60)
                plate_washed.push(0.0)
                plate_knock.push(0.0)
            }
        }
    }
    let plate_now: Vec<f32> = Vec::filled(plate.len(), value: 1.0)
    // The drum: the head's six modes, the stand-ins for its higher ones, the shell's three
    // and the snare-side head's.
    var skin: Vec<Mode> = Vec::new()
    var skin_hz: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var skin_t60: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    var skin_slow = [0.0; 6]
    var slap_share: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    if patch.head > 0.0 {
        var grain = seeded(seed + 55)
        for i in 0..6 {
            skin_hz.push(patch.head_hz * HEAD_RATIOS[i])
            skin_t60.push(patch.head_t60 * HEAD_RINGS[i])
            skin_slow[i] = 1.0 / sin(TAU * min(skin_hz[i], 0.45 * RATE) / RATE)
        }
        let more = log2(HEAD_MORE_TO / HEAD_MORE_FROM) / f32(HEAD_MORE)
        for k in 0..HEAD_MORE {
            let ratio = HEAD_MORE_FROM * exp2(more * (f32(k) + 0.5 + 0.4 * grain.next()))
            skin_hz.push(patch.head_hz * ratio)
            skin_t60.push(patch.head_t60 * clamp(0.83 * pow(8.8 / ratio, 0.55), 0.45, 1.0))
        }
        for i in 0..SHELL {
            skin_hz.push(patch.head_hz * SHELL_RATIOS[i])
            // (The wires shorten these too: that drum's 1 kHz octave is 6 dB lower from 100
            // to 300 ms with its wires on. By more here than its 838 Hz partial is shortened,
            // which falls 30 dB in 230 to 260 ms with them on: this drum's buzz ends sooner
            // than that one's, and a partial ringing on bare after it is the tin can.)
            skin_t60.push(SHELL_RINGS[i] * (1.0 - 0.55 * min(patch.wires, 1.0)))
        }
        let lowest = patch.head_hz * SNARE_FROM
        let step = log2(SNARE_TO / SNARE_FROM) / f32(SNARE_MODES)
        var squares = 0.0
        for k in 0..SNARE_MODES {
            let hz = lowest * exp2(step * (f32(k) + 0.5 + 0.4 * grain.next()))
            skin_hz.push(hz)
            // (A mode that takes T seconds to fall 60 dB is 2.2 / T Hz wide.)
            skin_t60.push(2.2 / (hz * (exp2(step) - 1.0)))
            // (Less at the two ends of the buzz.)
            let ends = min(1.0, 0.4 + 0.05 * f32(min(k + 1, SNARE_MODES - k)))
            let share = ends * pow(hz / lowest, SLAP_TILT)
            slap_share.push(share)
            squares += share * share
        }
        for k in 0..SNARE_MODES {
            slap_share[k] = slap_share[k] / sqrt(squares)
        }
        for i in 0..skin_hz.len() {
            var m = Mode {}
            m.tune(skin_hz[i], skin_t60[i])
            skin.push(m)
        }
    }
    let skin_blow: Vec<f32> = Vec::filled(skin.len(), value: 0.0)
    let plate_drift: Vec<f32> = Vec::filled(plate.len(), value: 0.0)
    let top_third = min(u32(3.0 * log2(max(patch.plate_high / patch.plate_low, 1.0))), 19)
    var third_takes = [0.0; 20]
    var plate_third: Vec<u32> = Vec::with_capacity(plate.len())
    for i in 0..plate.len() {
        let above = 3.0 * log2(max(plate_hz[i] / patch.plate_low, 1.0))
        let third = min(u32(above), 19)
        plate_third.push(third)
        let share = plate_washed[i] * plate_level[i]
        third_takes[third] += share * share
    }
    // (A round of trades is every 16 samples and moves half of the angle's square, a third
    // of the largest's on average: so the largest is the root of 96 times the share a sample.)
    var plate_turn: Vec<f32> = Vec::with_capacity(plate.len())
    for i in 0..plate.len() {
        let gap = max(PLATE_GAP, plate_hz[i] / PLATE_SHARE)
        plate_turn.push(sqrt(96.0 * CHURN * gap / RATE))
    }
    Drummer {
        patch,
        hits: [Hit {}; 2],
        next: 0,
        noise: seeded(seed),
        metal,
        band: band(patch.band_hz, patch.band_q),
        band_svf: Svf {},
        cut: band(hz: patch.cut_hz, q: 0.7),
        cut_svf: Svf {},
        top: band(hz: patch.top_hz, q: 0.7),
        top_svf: Svf {},
        soft: 1.0,
        soft_held: 0.0,
        click_held: 0.0,
        plate,
        plate_hz,
        plate_level,
        plate_over,
        plate_rings,
        plate_washed,
        ringing: 0,
        stopped: false,
        since: 1 << 30,
        weak: false,
        touch: seeded(seed + 31),
        skin,
        skin_hz,
        skin_t60,
        skin_blow,
        skin_slow,
        slap_share,
        contact: 0,
        contact_left: 0,
        stretched: 0.0,
        stretch_keeps: exp(-16.0 / (max(patch.stretch_time, 0.001) * RATE)),
        skin_in: 0,
        skin_live: false,
        skin_ref: max(patch.level * patch.head, 1e-6),
        wire: [0.0; 6],
        wire_v: [0.0; 6],
        head_was: 0.0,
        apart: [false; 6],
        plate_now,
        plate_drift,
        plate_third,
        thirds: [0.0; 20],
        plate_even,
        plate_knock,
        crowd: 0.0,
        trade_in: 0,
        trade_from: 0,
        trade_low,
        trade_high,
        plate_turn,
        top_third,
        third_takes,
        third_has: [0.0; 20],
        wander_in: 0,
        plate_ref: max(patch.level * patch.plate, 1e-6),
    }
}

/// How fast a hit that another is struck over dies away, and a choked one: seconds to fall
/// 60 dB.
const OVERTAKEN: f32 = 0.02
const CHOKED: f32 = 0.04

impl Drummer {
    /// The samples to a clap's next burst: the gap, a little more or less each time.
    @audio
    fn gap(mut self) -> u32 {
        let p = self.patch
        max(u32(p.burst_gap * (1.0 + p.burst_sway * self.noise.next()) * RATE), 1)
    }

    /// Strikes the drum `velocity` hard (0 to 1).
    @audio
    pub fn hit(mut self, velocity: f32) {
        let p = self.patch
        let v = clamp(velocity, 0.0, 1.0)
        // The hit before dies away under this one.
        let last = self.hits[1 - self.next]
        // In a roll (the hit before under 150 ms ago) each hit is its own.
        let rolling = self.since < u32(0.15 * RATE)
        self.weak = rolling && !self.weak
        self.since = 0
        var louder = 0.0
        var cents = 0.0
        var brighter = 1.0
        var spot = 0.0
        if rolling && p.hands > 0.0 {
            let lean = if self.weak { 1.0 } else { 0.0 }
            louder = p.hands * (self.touch.next() - 0.5 * lean)
            cents = p.hands * (10.0 * self.touch.next() - 3.0 * lean)
            brighter = exp2(p.hands * (0.4 * self.touch.next() - 0.15 * lean))
            spot = p.hands * self.touch.next()
        }
        let level = p.level * gain_of(-p.range_db * (1.0 - v) + louder)
        if last.on {
            self.hits[1 - self.next].fade_fall = min(last.fade_fall, decay(OVERTAKEN))
        }
        // A harder hit's noise rings longer.
        let rings = p.noise_t60 * (1.0 - p.ring_touch + 2.0 * p.ring_touch * v)
        // (A drum with wires has no noise of its own: its noise is the wires'.)
        let burst_level = if p.wires > 0.0 { 0.0 } else { level * (p.noise + p.metal) }
        let thump = if p.thump > 0.0 { max(u32(p.thump_ms * 0.001 * RATE), 2) } else { 0 }
        let first_gap = self.gap()
        // A skin still ringing from the blow before goes on from where it is: a blow adds to
        // its motion, it doesn't set it back to its start. (Every hit of a roll began the same
        // wave again from the same place: one hit over and over.)
        var phase = 0.0
        var phase2 = 0.0
        if last.on && last.tone + last.second > 0.02 * last.level * (p.tone + p.second) {
            phase = last.phase
            phase2 = last.phase2
        }
        self.hits[self.next] = Hit {
            on: true,
            level,
            phase,
            phase2,
            tuned: exp2((-p.tension * (1.0 - v) + cents) / 1200.0),
            tone: level * p.tone * (1.0 - 0.25 * spot),
            tone_fall: decay(p.tone_t60),
            second: level * p.second * (1.0 + 0.5 * spot),
            second_fall: decay(p.second_t60),
            sweep: p.sweep,
            sweep_fall: exp(-1.0 / (max(p.sweep_time, 0.0005) * RATE)),
            thump_all: thump,
            thump_left: thump,
            hiss: burst_level,
            hiss_fall: if p.bursts > 0 { decay(p.burst_t60) } else { decay(rings) },
            bursts: p.bursts,
            burst_in: first_gap,
            burst_level: burst_level * p.burst_fall,
            tail_fall: decay(rings),
            click: level * p.click,
            fade: 1.0,
            fade_fall: 1.0,
        }
        self.next = 1 - self.next
        self.soft = one_pole((p.soft_hz + v * v * (20000.0 - p.soft_hz)) * brighter)
        // The head: the stick lands at a place and with a weight of its own, and gives each
        // mode its share over its contact; the head is stretched, the more the harder.
        if p.head > 0.0 {
            let r = clamp(p.spot + p.spot_sway * self.touch.next(), 0.0, 0.95)
            let own = level * gain_of(0.7 * self.touch.next())
            let shares = spot_rings(r)
            for i in 0..6 {
                self.skin_blow[i] = own * p.head * HEAD_GAINS[i] * shares[i]
            }
            // The higher modes: each gets its share by where the stick landed, which no two
            // blows repeat.
            for k in 0..HEAD_MORE {
                let pick = self.touch.next()
                let way = if pick < 0.0 { -1.0 } else { 1.0 }
                self.skin_blow[6 + k] = way *
                    own *
                    p.head *
                    HEAD_MORE_GAIN *
                    (0.4 + 0.6 * abs(pick))
            }
            let shell = 6 + HEAD_MORE
            // The drum's long-ringing modes too: over a recorded drum's six hardest blows its
            // 838 Hz partial stands from 0 to 10 dB under its main one, and one that rings the
            // same every time is a tin can's. (0.72: that share's root mean square, so these
            // modes are as loud on the whole as they were fitted.)
            for i in 0..SHELL {
                let pick = self.touch.next()
                let share = (0.4 + 0.6 * abs(pick)) / 0.72
                self.skin_blow[shell + i] = own * p.shell * SHELL_GAINS[i] * share
            }
            // The snare-side head takes the blow through the air: every mode alike, one way
            // or the other.
            let air = own * p.wires * SNARE_AIR / sqrt(f32(SNARE_MODES))
            for k in 0..SNARE_MODES {
                let pick = self.touch.next()
                let way = if pick < 0.0 { -1.0 } else { 1.0 }
                self.skin_blow[shell + SHELL + k] = way * air * (0.4 + 0.6 * abs(pick))
            }
            let ms = p.contact_ms * (1.0 - 0.5 * v) * (1.0 + 0.1 * self.touch.next())
            self.contact = max(u32(ms * 0.001 * RATE), 2)
            self.contact_left = self.contact
            self.stretched = max(self.stretched, p.stretch * v * v)
            self.skin_live = true
        }
        // The plate: the blow sets every mode ringing, each a little more or less and one way
        // or the other (a stick never lands the same twice), a soft blow's high modes less;
        // what a crash's high modes get as a wash is fed to them over the next tenth of a
        // second, in `sound`.
        let modes = self.plate.len()
        if modes > 0 {
            if self.stopped {
                for i in 0..modes {
                    self.plate[i].tune(self.plate_hz[i], self.plate_rings[i])
                }
                self.stopped = false
            }
            let dull = -0.1151 * p.plate_touch * (1.0 - v)
            let knock = p.knock * gain_of(-p.knock_touch * (1.0 - v))
            for i in 0..modes {
                let over = self.plate_over[i]
                let washed = p.wash * self.plate_washed[i]
                let pick = self.noise.next()
                let way = if pick < 0.0 { -1.0 } else { 1.0 }
                // By the plate's shape a mode gets what of it isn't washed; of the stick's
                // own blow, the same as every mode, what is; and its share of the knock.
                let share = (1.0 - washed) * self.plate_level[i] +
                    washed * self.plate_even +
                    knock * self.plate_knock[i]
                let blow = level * p.plate * share * exp(dull * over)
                self.plate[i].strike(way * (0.5 + 0.5 * abs(pick)) * blow)
            }
            self.plate_ref = max(p.level * p.plate, 1e-6)
            self.ringing = modes
        }
    }

    /// Stops what rings, over a few milliseconds: a hand on a cymbal, or a closed hi-hat on an
    /// open one.
    @audio
    pub fn choke(mut self) {
        for i in 0..2 {
            self.hits[i].fade_fall = min(self.hits[i].fade_fall, decay(CHOKED))
        }
        if self.ringing > 0 && !self.stopped {
            for i in 0..self.plate.len() {
                self.plate[i].tune(self.plate_hz[i], CHOKED)
            }
            self.stopped = true
        }
    }

    /// Whether nothing of it sounds.
    @audio
    pub fn silent(self) -> bool {
        !self.hits[0].on && !self.hits[1].on && self.ringing == 0 && !self.skin_live
    }

    /// Adds its sound to samples `from` to `to` of `left` and `right`.
    @audio
    pub fn sound(mut self, left: mut [f32], right: mut [f32], from: u32, to: u32) {
        self.since = min(self.since + (to - from), 1 << 30)
        if self.silent() {
            return
        }
        let p = self.patch
        let hissing = p.noise > 0.0 || p.metal > 0.0
        let wired = p.wires > 0.0
        let shell = max(p.tone + p.second, 1e-6)
        // How much each third of an octave of a plate bends it, beside the third the stick's
        // knock is in (the second): the first's, and what each one up is of the one under.
        // What all a drum's wires lying on its head take of the head's swing a sample.
        let rested = 1.0 - decay(WIRE_REST)
        let bend_first = exp2(CASCADE_FALLS / 3.0)
        let bend_step = exp2(-CASCADE_FALLS / 3.0)
        for n in from..to {
            // The noise and the metal, before any hit shapes them.
            var source = 0.0
            if hissing {
                source = p.noise * self.noise.next()
                if p.metal > 0.0 {
                    var squares = 0.0
                    for i in 0..6 {
                        let dt = METAL_HZ[i] * p.metal_tune / RATE
                        squares += pulse(self.metal[i], dt, width: 0.5)
                        self.metal[i] = fract(self.metal[i] + dt)
                    }
                    source += p.metal * squares / 6.0
                }
                let banded = self.band_svf.pass(self.band, source)
                source = self.top_svf.low(self.top, self.cut_svf.high(self.cut, banded))
                self.soft_held += self.soft * (source - self.soft_held)
                source = self.soft_held
            }
            let raw = self.noise.next()
            self.click_held += 0.33 * (raw - self.click_held)
            var y = 0.0
            for h in 0..2 {
                if !self.hits[h].on {
                    continue
                }
                var hit = self.hits[h]
                // The tones: each a little above its pitch while the trigger's pulse lasts.
                var tones = 0.0
                // How far the skin is out, from -1 to 1: what the wires rattle with.
                var out = 0.0
                if hit.tone > 0.0 || hit.second > 0.0 {
                    let above = 1.0 + hit.sweep
                    tones = hit.tone * sin(TAU * hit.phase) + hit.second * sin(TAU * hit.phase2)
                    out = clamp(tones / (hit.level * shell), -1.0, 1.0)
                    hit.phase = fract(hit.phase + p.tone_hz * hit.tuned * above / RATE)
                    hit.phase2 = fract(hit.phase2 + p.second_hz * hit.tuned * above / RATE)
                    hit.tone *= hit.tone_fall
                    hit.second *= hit.second_fall
                    hit.sweep *= hit.sweep_fall
                }
                // The thump: a half sine, with the tones, into the drive.
                if hit.thump_left > 0 {
                    let through = f32(hit.thump_all - hit.thump_left) / f32(hit.thump_all)
                    tones += hit.level * p.thump * sin(PI * through)
                    hit.thump_left -= 1
                }
                if p.drive > 0.0 {
                    tones = warm(tones, p.drive)
                }
                // A clap's bursts: the noise starts again a few times, each a little under the
                // last, then its tail.
                if hit.bursts > 0 {
                    hit.burst_in -= 1
                    if hit.burst_in == 0 {
                        hit.hiss = hit.burst_level
                        hit.burst_level *= p.burst_fall
                        hit.bursts -= 1
                        hit.burst_in = self.gap()
                        if hit.bursts == 0 {
                            hit.hiss_fall = hit.tail_fall
                        }
                    }
                }
                let wires = hit.hiss * (1.0 + p.rattle * out)
                y += hit.fade * (tones + wires * source + hit.click * self.click_held)
                hit.hiss *= hit.hiss_fall
                // The click: gone in 5 ms.
                hit.click *= 0.9716
                hit.fade *= hit.fade_fall
                // Under -100 dB it's over.
                if (hit.tone + hit.second + hit.hiss + hit.click) * hit.fade < 1e-5 &&
                    hit.bursts == 0 &&
                    hit.thump_left == 0 {
                    hit.on = false
                }
                self.hits[h] = hit
            }
            // The drum: its modes ringing, the blow coming in over the stick's contact (a half
            // sine whose samples sum to 1), the head's pitch settling; and the wires.
            if self.skin_live {
                let heads = 6 + HEAD_MORE
                if self.skin_in == 0 {
                    self.skin_in = 16
                    if self.stretched > 0.0 {
                        if self.stretched < 0.3 {
                            self.stretched = 0.0
                        }
                        let up = exp2(self.stretched / 1200.0)
                        for i in 0..heads {
                            self.skin[i].tune(self.skin_hz[i] * up, self.skin_t60[i])
                        }
                        self.stretched *= self.stretch_keeps
                    }
                }
                self.skin_in -= 1
                var push = 0.0
                if self.contact_left > 0 {
                    let through = (f32(self.contact - self.contact_left) + 0.5) / f32(self.contact)
                    push = sin(PI * through) * PI / (2.0 * f32(self.contact))
                    self.contact_left -= 1
                }
                // (What moves the wires is the head's main mode and its second: a mode that
                // both moved them and was rung by their slaps would feed itself.)
                var head = 0.0
                var rest = 0.0
                for i in 0..self.skin.len() {
                    if push != 0.0 {
                        self.skin[i].strike(push * self.skin_blow[i])
                    }
                    let out = self.skin[i].next()
                    if i < 2 {
                        head += out
                    } else if i >= heads && i + 1 < heads + SHELL {
                        // (The drum's long-ringing modes move the wires a little too, and
                        // sound whole.)
                        head += WIRE_LONG * out
                        rest += (1.0 - WIRE_LONG) * out
                    } else {
                        rest += out
                    }
                }
                y += head + rest
                if wired {
                    // The wires: each strand pulled toward the head by its spring, carried by
                    // the head while it lies on it, thrown off when the head swings back
                    // faster than it follows, and slapping the head when it is caught again,
                    // then bouncing off it with some of the speed it landed with until it
                    // lands too slowly to. A slap rings the snare-side head's modes and the
                    // drum's low ones but its main one, each mode its own share of it (a
                    // strand lands along its length, never the same way twice), and the
                    // head gives the strand its speed: the buzz is the drum ringing, and the
                    // head pays for it.
                    let at = head / self.skin_ref
                    let moving = at - self.head_was
                    self.head_was = at
                    var took = 0.0
                    var resting = 0.0
                    for w in 0..WIRES {
                        let along = f32(w) / f32(WIRES - 1)
                        let pull = WIRE_PULL * (0.6 + 0.8 * along)
                        let hold = p.wires_hold * (0.7 + 0.6 * along)
                        var v = self.wire_v[w]
                        v += -pull * (self.wire[w] + hold) - WIRE_DRAG * v
                        var place = self.wire[w] + v
                        if place <= at {
                            let slap = moving - v
                            let lands = self.apart[w] && slap > 0.0
                            if lands {
                                took += (1.0 + WIRE_BOUNCE) * slap
                                let kick = self.skin_ref *
                                    p.wires *
                                    SLAP_GAIN *
                                    pow(slap, SLAP_CURVE)
                                // (The head's third and fourth modes, and the last of the
                                // drum's other modes, which is that head's own.)
                                self.skin[2].strike(SLAP_LOWS[0] * kick * self.noise.next())
                                self.skin[3].strike(SLAP_LOWS[1] * kick * self.noise.next())
                                let own = heads + SHELL - 1
                                self.skin[own].strike(SLAP_LOWS[2] * kick * self.noise.next())
                                for k in 0..SNARE_MODES {
                                    let share = self.slap_share[k] * self.noise.next()
                                    self.skin[heads + SHELL + k].strike(kick * share)
                                }
                            }
                            place = at
                            if lands && slap > WIRE_STILL {
                                v = moving + WIRE_BOUNCE * slap
                            } else {
                                v = moving
                                self.apart[w] = false
                                resting += 1.0
                            }
                        } else {
                            self.apart[w] = true
                        }
                        self.wire[w] = place
                        self.wire_v[w] = v
                    }
                    // (A change in a mode's speed is a strike of that over the sine of its
                    // turn a sample.) And the strands lying on the head damp it.
                    // No more than half of the head's speed in a sample.
                    let slower = min(
                        WIRE_LOAD / f32(WIRES) * took,
                        0.5 * abs(moving),
                    ) *
                        self.skin_ref
                    let keeps = 1.0 - rested * resting / f32(WIRES)
                    for i in 0..6 {
                        if took > 0.0 && i < 2 {
                            self.skin[i].strike(-WIRE_SHARES[i] * slower * self.skin_slow[i])
                        }
                        self.skin[i].lose(keeps)
                    }
                }
            }
            // The plate: its modes ringing, each at a strength that may wander, each trading
            // with the next if the blow has set them to. The modes are also taken a third of
            // an octave at a time, and each third is pushed by the square of the plate's
            // bending under it: energy leaves the stick's knock and the low modes for the
            // modes above, fast while they are large and slowly after, as the anechoic
            // crash's 500 Hz octave gives way to its 2, 4 and 8 kHz ones.
            if self.ringing > 0 {
                if self.wander_in == 0 {
                    self.wander_in = 256
                    if p.restless > 0.0 {
                        for i in 0..self.ringing {
                            let was = self.plate_drift[i]
                            let now = 0.97 * was + 0.4 * p.restless * self.noise.next()
                            self.plate_drift[i] = now
                            self.plate_now[i] = exp(clamp(now, -2.3, 1.2))
                        }
                    }
                }
                self.wander_in -= 1
                // The crowd: how far the modes are set trading rises with how loud the plate
                // is and falls away of itself; every 16 samples each mode trades with the next
                // (the pairs taken from the first mode and from the second by turns), so no
                // energy is made or lost by it but each mode is a band of noise, not a tone,
                // and what one part of the plate has spreads to the rest. (Each mode giving
                // up a share and getting as much back in pushes sized by its third's energy
                // lost the plate's top 27 dB in half a second: pushes sized by what they feed
                // keep it only on average, and what holds on average drains away.)
                if p.crowd > 0.0 {
                    if self.trade_in == 0 {
                        self.trade_in = 16
                        var all = 0.0
                        for b in 0..20 {
                            all += self.third_has[b]
                        }
                        let loud = all / (self.plate_ref * self.plate_ref)
                        let more = min(CROWD_RATE * 16.0 / RATE * sqrt(loud), 1.0)
                        let less = 16.0 / (CROWD_STAYS * RATE)
                        self.crowd += (1.0 - self.crowd) * more - self.crowd * less
                        let wide = sqrt(p.crowd * self.crowd)
                        // (A ping's two modes and a tick's stand apart: they don't trade.)
                        let last = min(self.ringing, self.trade_high)
                        var i = self.trade_low + self.trade_from
                        while i + 1 < last {
                            var mine = self.plate[i]
                            let turn = wide * self.plate_turn[i] * self.noise.next()
                            self.plate[i + 1] = mine.trade(self.plate[i + 1], turn)
                            self.plate[i] = mine
                            i += 2
                        }
                        self.trade_from = 1 - self.trade_from
                    }
                    self.trade_in -= 1
                }
                // What each third is pushed by, and what the modes under it lose for it. The
                // bending under a third is all that sounded, a sample ago, two thirds or more
                // below it, each third counted the less the higher it is; the push is the
                // square of that, its size and sign drawn anew each sample (the square alone
                // is a few steady lines, and a line that falls on a mode pumps that one mode
                // without end: one partial at 6.35 kHz came to carry most of a crash). A mode
                // pushed at random gains the push's square in energy (its swing squared), a
                // third of the largest push's on average, so a third gains that times the
                // squares of its modes' shares: that much comes off what bends it, each third
                // under it losing by its part in the bending, and no third is passed more
                // than a 750th of that in one sample.
                var drives = [0.0; 20]
                var keep = [1.0; 20]
                if p.wash > 0.0 {
                    var bends = [0.0; 20]
                    var asked = [0.0; 20]
                    var bend = bend_first
                    var bent = 0.0
                    var had = 0.0
                    for b in 2..self.top_third + 1 {
                        bends[b - 2] = bend
                        bent += bend * self.thirds[b - 2]
                        had += bend * bend * self.third_has[b - 2]
                        bend *= bend_step
                        let takes = self.third_takes[b]
                        if takes <= 0.0 || had <= 1e-12 {
                            continue
                        }
                        let held = clamp(bent, -1.5, 1.5)
                        var push = p.wash * CASCADE * self.plate_ref * held * held
                        if push * push * takes > 0.004 * had {
                            push = sqrt(0.004 * had / takes)
                        }
                        drives[b] = push * self.noise.next()
                        asked[b] = push * push * takes / (3.0 * had)
                    }
                    var owed = 0.0
                    var b = self.top_third
                    while b >= 2 {
                        owed += asked[b]
                        keep[b - 2] = 1.0 - 0.5 * bends[b - 2] * bends[b - 2] * owed
                        b -= 1
                    }
                }
                var thirds = [0.0; 20]
                var has = [0.0; 20]
                var plate = 0.0
                for i in 0..self.ringing {
                    let b = self.plate_third[i]
                    let drive = drives[b]
                    if drive != 0.0 {
                        // (Each mode takes it as the plate's shape gives it its level.)
                        self.plate[i].strike(drive * self.plate_washed[i] * self.plate_level[i])
                    }
                    if keep[b] < 1.0 {
                        self.plate[i].lose(keep[b])
                    }
                    let rung = self.plate[i].next()
                    has[b] += 2.0 * rung * rung
                    let out = rung * self.plate_now[i]
                    plate += out
                    thirds[b] += out / self.plate_ref
                }
                if p.wash > 0.0 || p.crowd > 0.0 {
                    self.thirds = thirds
                    for b in 0..20 {
                        self.third_has[b] += 0.02 * (has[b] - self.third_has[b])
                    }
                }
                y += plate
            }
            left[n] += y
            right[n] += y
        }
        // A head that has died away, its wires still, is over.
        if self.skin_live && self.contact_left == 0 {
            var rings = 0.0
            for i in 0..self.skin.len() {
                rings += self.skin[i].level()
            }
            if rings < 1e-5 {
                self.skin_live = false
                self.wire = [0.0; 6]
                self.wire_v = [0.0; 6]
                self.head_was = 0.0
                self.apart = [false; 6]
            }
        }
        // The plate's modes that have died away (the highest die first) are left out.
        while self.ringing > 0 && self.plate[self.ringing - 1].level() < 1e-6 {
            self.ringing -= 1
        }
        if self.ringing == 0 {
            self.crowd = 0.0
        }
    }
}

// ---- the kit --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A bass drum: a boom at 52 Hz that starts two octaves up and is down in a few milliseconds,
/// set off by a 2 ms pulse and driven a little, so a small speaker plays its attack and its
/// harmonics.
pub const KICK: Drum = Drum {
    tone_hz: 52.0,
    tone: 1.0,
    tone_t60: 0.42,
    sweep: 3.0,
    sweep_time: 0.014,
    drive: 2.4,
    thump: 0.9,
    thump_ms: 2.0,
    click: 0.1,
    range_db: 14.0,
    level: 1.7,
}

/// A snare: a skin at 185 and 396 Hz, and its wires rattling with it, in a band round 1.5 kHz
/// that the top is taken from.
pub const SNARE: Drum = Drum {
    tone_hz: 185.0,
    tone: 0.8,
    tone_t60: 0.17,
    sweep: 0.35,
    sweep_time: 0.012,
    second_hz: 396.0,
    second: 0.5,
    second_t60: 0.1,
    drive: 1.0,
    noise: 1.3,
    band_hz: 1500.0,
    band_q: 0.55,
    cut_hz: 250.0,
    top_hz: 7000.0,
    noise_t60: 0.22,
    rattle: 0.7,
    click: 0.15,
    range_db: 22.0,
    soft_hz: 3500.0,
    ring_touch: 0.25,
    tension: 25.0,
    hands: 1.0,
}

/// A clap: noise round 1.1 kHz, three bursts 8 to 13 ms apart, each under the last, and a tail.
pub const CLAP: Drum = Drum {
    noise: 1.0,
    band_hz: 1150.0,
    band_q: 1.6,
    cut_hz: 500.0,
    top_hz: 8000.0,
    noise_t60: 0.28,
    bursts: 3,
    burst_gap: 0.0105,
    burst_sway: 0.25,
    burst_t60: 0.035,
    burst_fall: 0.8,
    range_db: 20.0,
}

/// A closed hi-hat: the six squares and a little noise, high and short; shorter and duller
/// when it is struck softly.
pub const HAT: Drum = Drum {
    metal: 1.0,
    metal_tune: 1.0,
    noise: 0.35,
    band_hz: 8200.0,
    band_q: 0.9,
    cut_hz: 6500.0,
    noise_t60: 0.055,
    range_db: 24.0,
    soft_hz: 6000.0,
    ring_touch: 0.35,
}

/// An open hi-hat: the closed one, ringing on. A closed hit chokes it.
pub const OPEN_HAT: Drum = Drum { noise_t60: 0.55, ring_touch: 0.0, ..HAT }

/// A snare drum, fitted to one recorded close and dry (Virtuosity Drums' snare microphone:
/// docs/citations.md): a head whose main mode is 226 Hz, struck near its centre, its higher
/// modes, a snare-side head and wires on it, all one drum. The stick's crack is the head's
/// own modes; the buzz is the wires' slaps ringing the snare-side head, 18 dB under the tone
/// at first and as loud as it from 30 ms on; and the head pays for the buzz (its 250 Hz
/// octave falls 30 dB in 60 ms, as that drum's does with its wires on; 130 ms with them off).
/// Hard, each octave from 250 Hz to 8 kHz is within 6 dB of that drum's through its first
/// 300 ms. Softer, its buzz is 5 to 7 dB over that drum's in its first 100 ms, and its tail
/// is gone sooner (50 dB down in 0.2 s, where that drum's takes 0.4 s). Where `SNARE` is a
/// drum machine's circuit, this is a drum: its hits are each their own.
pub const SNARE_DRUM: Drum = Drum {
    head: 1.0,
    head_hz: 226.0,
    head_t60: 0.30,
    spot: 0.12,
    spot_sway: 0.08,
    stretch: 35.0,
    stretch_time: 0.04,
    contact_ms: 0.5,
    shell: 1.0,
    wires: 1.0,
    wires_hold: 0.005,
    range_db: 24.0,
    level: 0.65,
}

/// A crash cymbal: every mode of the plate up to 20 kHz struck at once, with the stick's
/// tick on top (modes round 9 kHz, gone in a few tenths of a second), so it is loudest at
/// the stick and its top dies first; the stick's knock in the plate's own modes round 410 Hz,
/// whose energy the plate's bending passes up to the modes round 4 kHz over the next tenth
/// of a second (a small bloom, 1 to 2 dB in that octave); and the modes trading, so it is a
/// wash of noise with partials in it, a soft one a little less so. Its modes ring as the
/// 18-inch crash's it follows do (9.5 s at 1 kHz and under, 3 s at 8 kHz, 1 s at 16 kHz),
/// every one 13.7 dB a second faster: 3 s at 1 kHz, under 2 s at 8 kHz, under 1 s at 16 kHz.
/// (Struck hard on its shoulder that crash's knock is 13 dB over its 4 kHz octave at the
/// stick and its octaves over 2 kHz bloom 9 dB, a tenth of a second after; struck less hard,
/// or on its bow, they are loudest at the stick, as this one's are. With most of the blow
/// held back for the bloom, this one's top swelled 20 dB over a quarter of a second, and was
/// heard as a crash played backwards. Its first 5 ms are spread as that crash's are when
/// struck less hard: 6, 13, 7, 6 and 7 dB under the whole from 250 Hz to 16 kHz by octaves
/// and pairs of them, against 8, 13, 7, 3 and 10.)
pub const CRASH: Drum = Drum {
    plate: 1.0,
    plate_high: 20000.0,
    plate_hz: 4000.0,
    plate_under: 8.0,
    plate_over: 10.0,
    plate_t60: 9.5,
    plate_damp: 0.55,
    plate_fade: 13.7,
    wash: 1.0,
    crowd: 1.0,
    knock: 1.3,
    knock_hz: 410.0,
    knock_touch: 20.0,
    tick: 1.0,
    tick_hz: 9000.0,
    tick_t60: 0.3,
    range_db: 20.0,
    level: 0.28,
}

/// A ride cymbal: the stick's tick (modes round 14.5 kHz, gone in a tenth of a second), a
/// note at 2.5 kHz, a low body in the plate's own modes from 280 to 1120 Hz, most round
/// 560 Hz, and a plate loudest at 8 kHz, quieter in between, its modes trading a little. Its
/// modes ring as the 21-inch ride's it follows do (12 s at 1 kHz and under, 2 s at 8 kHz),
/// every one 10 dB a second faster: 4 s and 1.5 s. Beside its 4 to 8 kHz octaves its 500 Hz
/// octave is 4 dB under at 0.1 s, 3 dB over at 0.5 s and 15 dB over at 1.5 s, where that
/// ride's is 5 to 7 under, 0 to 3 over and 13 to 15 over; and its 10 to 20 kHz is 4 dB over
/// in its first 20 ms and 10 dB under at 0.1 s, where that ride's is 7 to 8 over and 8 to 10
/// under. (The tick is 8 dB stronger beside the plate than it was, and the level lower to
/// leave it room: a hard stroke peaks just under full scale.)
pub const RIDE: Drum = Drum {
    plate: 0.5,
    plate_low: 340.0,
    plate_high: 16000.0,
    plate_hz: 8000.0,
    plate_under: 5.0,
    plate_over: 12.0,
    plate_t60: 12.0,
    plate_damp: 0.86,
    plate_fade: 10.0,
    ping: 0.25,
    ping_hz: 2500.0,
    ping_t60: 1.6,
    restless: 0.15,
    crowd: 0.1,
    knock: 0.5,
    knock_hz: 560.0,
    knock_wide: 1.0,
    tick: 20.0,
    tick_hz: 14500.0,
    tick_t60: 0.12,
    click: 0.15,
    range_db: 18.0,
    level: 0.065,
}

/// A rim shot: a short knock at 430 and 1100 Hz with the stick's click.
pub const RIM: Drum = Drum {
    tone_hz: 430.0,
    tone: 0.7,
    tone_t60: 0.045,
    second_hz: 1100.0,
    second: 0.5,
    second_t60: 0.03,
    click: 0.5,
    range_db: 20.0,
}

// The tests render on the program's thread, as the audio thread would.

/// The energy of `seconds` of `d` after one hit, and of its sample-to-sample differences (the
/// highs).
fn rendered(d: mut Drummer, seconds: f32) -> vec2 {
    var e = vec2(0.0)
    var last = 0.0
    var l = [0.0; 128]
    var r = [0.0; 128]
    for _ in 0..u32(seconds * RATE) / 128 {
        for i in 0..128 {
            l[i] = 0.0
            r[i] = 0.0
        }
        d.sound(mut l, mut r, from: 0, to: 128)
        for i in 0..128 {
            e += vec2(l[i] * l[i], (l[i] - last) * (l[i] - last))
            last = l[i]
        }
    }
    e
}

@test
fn a_kick_is_low_a_hat_is_high_and_both_die_away() {
    var kick = drummer(KICK)
    kick.hit(1.0)
    let k = rendered(mut kick, seconds: 0.25)
    var hat = drummer(HAT)
    hat.hit(1.0)
    let h = rendered(mut hat, seconds: 0.25)
    // The highs' share of each: a kick's sample-to-sample differences are tiny beside it, a
    // hat's larger than it.
    assert(k.x > 1.0 && k.y / k.x < 0.01, f"a kick's highs are {k.y / k.x} of it")
    assert(h.x > 1e-3 && h.y / h.x > 1.0, f"a hat's highs are {h.y / h.x} of it")
    let _ = rendered(mut kick, seconds: 1.5)
    let _ = rendered(mut hat, seconds: 0.5)
    assert(kick.silent() && hat.silent())
}

@test
fn a_soft_hit_is_quieter_and_a_choke_stops_an_open_hat() {
    var loud = drummer(SNARE)
    loud.hit(1.0)
    var soft = drummer(SNARE)
    soft.hit(0.3)
    let ratio = rendered(mut loud, seconds: 0.3).x / rendered(mut soft, seconds: 0.3).x
    assert(ratio > 10.0, f"a hard snare has {ratio} times a soft one's energy")
    var open = drummer(OPEN_HAT)
    open.hit(1.0)
    let _ = rendered(mut open, seconds: 0.05)
    open.choke()
    let _ = rendered(mut open, seconds: 0.2)
    assert(open.silent(), "a choked hat rang on")
}

@test
fn a_snares_wires_rattle_with_its_skin() {
    // The same snare with the rattle and without, over the 40 ms its skin rings loudest: with
    // it, the noise's level moves with the skin, so the two differ.
    var with = drummer(SNARE, seed: 3)
    with.hit(1.0)
    var without = drummer(Drum { rattle: 0.0, ..SNARE }, seed: 3)
    without.hit(1.0)
    let a = rendered(mut with, seconds: 0.04)
    let b = rendered(mut without, seconds: 0.04)
    assert(
        abs(a.y - b.y) > 0.02 * b.y,
        f"the rattle changed the snare's highs by {abs(a.y - b.y) / b.y}",
    )
}

/// A cymbal is a plate of modes: it rings for seconds, its top dies before its bottom, and a
/// hand stops it. A second and a half after a crash it still sounds, with a smaller share of
/// highs than it had in its first third of a second.
@test
fn a_cymbal_rings_on_its_top_dies_first_and_a_hand_stops_it() {
    var crash = drummer(CRASH)
    crash.hit(1.0)
    let _ = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.1)
    let early = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.2)
    let _ = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 1.2)
    let late = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.2)
    assert(late.x > 1e-4 && !crash.silent(), "a crash died within a second and a half")
    assert(
        late.y / late.x < 0.9 * early.y / early.x,
        f"a crash's highs: {early.y / early.x} of it early, {late.y / late.x} late",
    )
    crash.choke()
    let _ = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.3)
    assert(crash.silent(), "a choked crash rang on")
}

/// A crash is loudest at the stick: every mode is struck at once and what blooms after is
/// small, so 40 ms a tenth of a second in have no more than a quarter over the first 40 ms'
/// energy, or highs, and 40 ms half a second in under half. (With most of the blow held back
/// for the bloom its top swelled 20 dB over a quarter of a second, and it was heard as a crash
/// played backwards.) And a ride is the stick's tick first and then far softer.
@test
fn a_crash_is_loudest_at_the_stick_and_a_ride_ticks() {
    var crash = drummer(CRASH)
    crash.hit(1.0)
    let first = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.04)
    let _ = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.06)
    let bloom = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.04)
    let _ = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.36)
    let later = rendered(mut crash, seconds: 0.04)
    assert(
        bloom.x < 1.25 * first.x && bloom.y < 1.25 * first.y,
        f"a crash's energy and highs: {first} at the stick, {bloom} a tenth of a second in",
    )
    assert(later.x < 0.5 * first.x, f"a crash: {first.x} at the stick, {later.x} at 0.5 s")
    var ride = drummer(RIDE)
    ride.hit(1.0)
    let tick = rendered(mut ride, seconds: 0.02).x
    let _ = rendered(mut ride, seconds: 0.08)
    let rings = rendered(mut ride, seconds: 0.02).x
    assert(
        tick > 5.0 * rings && rings > 0.0,
        f"a ride's energy: {tick} at the stick, {rings} later",
    )
}

/// A plate's low body is several of its own modes, and they ring no longer than its middle:
/// the crash's knock is shared by five modes or more (their squares summing to 1), and the
/// ride's modes at 340 Hz and at 1 kHz both ring 4 s (12 s, every mode 10 dB a second faster),
/// its 8 kHz ones 1.5 s. (As one tone of its own the crash's knock stood 42 dB over the
/// middle of its octave, and the ride's low modes rang seven to eleven seconds over a top
/// gone in one: the two were heard as part bell and part gong.)
@test
fn a_plates_low_body_is_several_of_its_modes_and_rings_no_longer_than_its_middle() {
    let crash = drummer(CRASH)
    var sharing = 0
    var squares = 0.0
    for i in 0..crash.plate_knock.len() {
        let share = crash.plate_knock[i]
        squares += share * share
        if share > 0.1 {
            sharing += 1
        }
    }
    assert(sharing >= 5 && abs(squares - 1.0) < 1e-4, f"{sharing} modes share the knock")
    let low = plate_ring(RIDE, 340.0)
    let mid = plate_ring(RIDE, 1000.0)
    let top = plate_ring(RIDE, 8000.0)
    assert(low == mid && abs(mid - 4.0) < 0.05, f"the ride rings {low} s at 340 Hz, {mid} at 1 kHz")
    assert(abs(top - 1.5) < 0.05, f"the ride rings {top} s at 8 kHz")
}

/// Over 1 kHz the gaps between a plate's modes are of every size: of the crash's, more than a
/// tenth are under a quarter of the usual gap and more than a twentieth over twice it. (Each
/// near its place, no two lay close enough to beat slowly, and each third of an octave of
/// the plate fell like one tone with an envelope: its level moved 0.3 dB from one twentieth
/// of a second to the next where the anechoic crash's moves 0.6 to 1.0.)
@test
fn a_plates_modes_over_1_khz_lie_at_gaps_of_every_size() {
    let crash = drummer(CRASH)
    var close = 0
    var far = 0
    var all = 0
    for i in crash.trade_low..crash.trade_high - 1 {
        let hz = crash.plate_hz[i]
        if hz < 1000.0 {
            continue
        }
        let gap = (crash.plate_hz[i + 1] - hz) / max(PLATE_GAP, hz / PLATE_SHARE)
        all += 1
        if gap < 0.25 {
            close += 1
        }
        if gap > 2.0 {
            far += 1
        }
    }
    assert(all > 100, f"the crash has {all} gaps over 1 kHz")
    assert(
        10 * close > all && 20 * far > all,
        f"of {all} gaps, {close} are under a quarter of the usual and {far} over twice it",
    )
}

/// A hard crash's modes trade, and trading makes no energy and loses none: from 0.3 s to 0.8 s
/// the crash has what the same crash has with its modes left alone, to within a quarter, in
/// its highs too. (Modes that gave up a share and were pushed back as much on average lost
/// the top 27 dB in half a second.) And they do trade: the two are not one sound.
@test
fn a_crashs_modes_trade_without_making_or_losing_energy() {
    var trading = drummer(CRASH, seed: 5)
    trading.hit(1.0)
    var alone = drummer(Drum { crowd: 0.0, ..CRASH }, seed: 5)
    alone.hit(1.0)
    let _ = rendered(mut trading, seconds: 0.3)
    let _ = rendered(mut alone, seconds: 0.3)
    let a = rendered(mut trading, seconds: 0.5)
    let b = rendered(mut alone, seconds: 0.5)
    assert(a.x > 0.8 * b.x && a.x < 1.25 * b.x, f"trading, a crash has {a.x}; alone, {b.x}")
    assert(a.y > 0.8 * b.y && a.y < 1.25 * b.y, f"trading, its highs are {a.y}; alone, {b.y}")
    assert(a.x != b.x, "a hard crash's modes did not trade")
}

/// How many times `seconds` of `d` cross zero going up.
fn crossings(d: mut Drummer, seconds: f32) -> u32 {
    var n = 0
    var last = 0.0
    var l = [0.0; 128]
    var r = [0.0; 128]
    for _ in 0..u32(seconds * RATE) / 128 {
        for i in 0..128 {
            l[i] = 0.0
            r[i] = 0.0
        }
        d.sound(mut l, mut r, from: 0, to: 128)
        for i in 0..128 {
            if last <= 0.0 && l[i] > 0.0 {
                n += 1
            }
            last = l[i]
        }
    }
    n
}

/// A skin's tone alone, ringing two seconds, a semitone lower for the softest blow.
const SKIN: Drum = Drum { tone_hz: 200.0, tone: 1.0, tone_t60: 2.0, tension: 100.0 }

/// A hard blow stretches the skin, so it sounds higher than a soft one; and a skin struck again
/// while it rings goes on from where it is. (Each hit of a roll used to start the same wave
/// from the same place: the first 12 ms of consecutive hits were 0.98 alike, and are 0.29
/// now.) Struck a second time a quarter of a turn into its wave, the skin is not at its start:
/// its first sample after the blow is far from 0.
@test
fn a_hard_blow_is_higher_and_a_roll_goes_on_from_where_the_skin_is() {
    var hard = drummer(SKIN)
    hard.hit(1.0)
    var soft = drummer(SKIN)
    soft.hit(0.0)
    let (h, s) = (crossings(mut hard, seconds: 1.0), crossings(mut soft, seconds: 1.0))
    assert(h >= 198 && h <= 202, f"the hardest blow sounds at {h} Hz")
    assert(s >= 187 && s <= 191, f"the softest blow sounds at {s} Hz, a semitone under 200")
    // 61 samples is a quarter of a turn at 200 Hz (a little more).
    var again = drummer(SKIN)
    again.hit(1.0)
    var l = [0.0; 128]
    var r = [0.0; 128]
    again.sound(mut l, mut r, from: 0, to: 61)
    again.hit(1.0)
    again.sound(mut l, mut r, from: 61, to: 62)
    assert(abs(l[61]) > 0.5, f"struck again a quarter of a turn in, the next sample is {l[61]}")
}

/// A knock a few hundredths of a second long, a roll's hits as unlike as they get.
const KNOCK: Drum = Drum { tone_hz: 300.0, tone: 1.0, tone_t60: 0.03, hands: 1.0 }

/// The energies of four hits of `d`: three 60 ms apart (a roll), and one a third of a second
/// after them (a hit by itself).
fn rolled(d: mut Drummer) -> [f32; 4] {
    var out = [0.0; 4]
    for k in 0..3 {
        d.hit(1.0)
        out[k] = rendered(mut d, seconds: 0.06).x
    }
    let _ = rendered(mut d, seconds: 0.3)
    d.hit(1.0)
    out[3] = rendered(mut d, seconds: 0.06).x
    out
}

/// In a roll each hit is its own: the second and third of three hits 60 ms apart differ from
/// the first and from each other (each within 1.5 dB of it), by seeded draws, so the same
/// drummer plays the same roll again. A hit by itself takes no draw: it is the first again.
@test
fn a_rolls_hits_are_each_their_own_and_a_hit_alone_is_as_it_was() {
    var d = drummer(KNOCK, seed: 4)
    let a = rolled(mut d)
    for k in 1..3 {
        let off = abs(4.3429448 * log(a[k] / a[0]))
        assert(off > 0.02 && off < 3.0, f"hit {k} of the roll is {off} dB from the first")
    }
    assert(abs(a[1] - a[2]) > 0.002 * a[0], "the roll's second and third hits are the same")
    assert(abs(a[3] - a[0]) < 0.001 * a[0], f"a hit alone: {a[3] / a[0]} of the first")
    var again = drummer(KNOCK, seed: 4)
    let b = rolled(mut again)
    assert(a[1] == b[1] && a[2] == b[2], "the same drummer played another roll")
}

/// The energy of the next `seconds` of `d`'s sample-to-sample differences: its highs.
fn highs(d: mut Drummer, seconds: f32) -> f32 {
    rendered(mut d, seconds).y
}

/// A snare drum's buzz is its wires answering its head: with wires its first tenth of a second
/// has over twice the highs it has without them (most of the stick's crack is the head's own:
/// a recorded drum's 4 kHz octave is 7 dB up with its wires on from 10 to 30 ms); held so
/// hard against the head that it never throws them (a hold of a hundred times the hardest
/// blow's swing), they are still, and add next to nothing; and 0.3 s on there is no buzz
/// left, where a steady band of noise would still be dying. And the head pays for the buzz:
/// from 0.1 to 0.3 s the drum has under half the energy with its wires on that it has with
/// them off (a recorded drum's 250 Hz octave falls 30 dB in 60 ms with its wires on and
/// 130 ms with them off).
@test
fn a_drums_wires_buzz_while_its_head_throws_them_and_load_it() {
    var with = drummer(SNARE_DRUM, seed: 2)
    with.hit(1.0)
    var without = drummer(Drum { wires: 0.0, ..SNARE_DRUM }, seed: 2)
    without.hit(1.0)
    var held = drummer(Drum { wires_hold: 100.0, ..SNARE_DRUM }, seed: 2)
    held.hit(1.0)
    let (a, b, c) = (
        highs(mut with, seconds: 0.1),
        highs(mut without, seconds: 0.1),
        highs(mut held, seconds: 0.1),
    )
    assert(a > 2.5 * b, f"its highs with wires are {a / b} times those without")
    assert(c < 0.5 * a, f"held hard, the wires give {c / a} of the highs they give free")
    let on = rendered(mut with, seconds: 0.2)
    let off = rendered(mut without, seconds: 0.2).x
    assert(on.x < 0.5 * off, f"from 0.1 to 0.3 s: {on.x} with its wires on, {off} with them off")
    let late = highs(mut with, seconds: 0.1)
    assert(late < 0.001 * a, f"0.3 s on, its highs are {late / a} of its first tenth's")
}

/// The energy a narrow listener at `hz` (two modes in a row, each 20 Hz wide) picks out of the
/// next `seconds` of `d`.
fn heard_at(d: mut Drummer, seconds: f32, hz: f32) -> f32 {
    var ear = Mode {}
    ear.tune(hz, 0.11)
    var second = Mode {}
    second.tune(hz, 0.11)
    var e = 0.0
    var l = [0.0; 128]
    var r = [0.0; 128]
    for _ in 0..u32(seconds * RATE) / 128 {
        for i in 0..128 {
            l[i] = 0.0
            r[i] = 0.0
        }
        d.sound(mut l, mut r, from: 0, to: 128)
        for i in 0..128 {
            ear.strike(l[i])
            second.strike(0.01 * ear.next())
            let y = second.next()
            e += y * y
        }
    }
    e
}

/// A snare drum's partials stand in its buzz. Between its 838 and 1190 Hz partials (at 4.4
/// times its head's main mode, where the only modes it has are the snare-side head's) a
/// narrow listener hears, from 30 to 150 ms, over a thirtieth of what it hears at the 838 Hz
/// one: a sixth as the drum is, 8 dB under. (With no buzz under 1.8 kHz it heard a 450th,
/// 27 dB under: those partials rang bare, 20 to 27 dB over the middle of their thirds of an
/// octave where a recorded drum's stand 9 to 15, and the drum was heard as a tin can.)
@test
fn a_snare_drums_partials_stand_in_its_buzz() {
    var a = drummer(SNARE_DRUM, seed: 2)
    a.hit(1.0)
    var b = drummer(SNARE_DRUM, seed: 2)
    b.hit(1.0)
    let _ = rendered(mut a, seconds: 0.03)
    let _ = rendered(mut b, seconds: 0.03)
    let partial = heard_at(mut a, seconds: 0.12, hz: SHELL_RATIOS[0] * SNARE_DRUM.head_hz)
    let between = heard_at(mut b, seconds: 0.12, hz: 4.4 * SNARE_DRUM.head_hz)
    assert(between > 0.03 * partial, f"between its partials: {between / partial} of the partial")
}

/// A snare drum's wires never keep themselves going: after a hard blow, after a soft one and
/// after a roll of eight, the drum is silent within a second and a half. (Slaps that rang the
/// modes that move the wires fed themselves: the buzz grew for half a second after one blow.)
@test
fn a_snare_drum_falls_silent_after_a_blow_and_after_a_roll() {
    var d = drummer(SNARE_DRUM, seed: 6)
    d.hit(1.0)
    let _ = rendered(mut d, seconds: 1.5)
    assert(d.silent(), "the drum rang on a second and a half after a hard blow")
    d.hit(0.2)
    let _ = rendered(mut d, seconds: 1.5)
    assert(d.silent(), "the drum rang on a second and a half after a soft blow")
    for _ in 0..8 {
        d.hit(0.8)
        let _ = rendered(mut d, seconds: 0.06)
    }
    let _ = rendered(mut d, seconds: 1.5)
    assert(d.silent(), "the drum rang on a second and a half after a roll")
}

/// A blow at the centre rings the head's first and fourth modes alone (the two with no line
/// across the head); halfway out it rings the second fully. And no two blows of a drum are the
/// same, a lone one included: each lands at a place and a weight of its own, where the drum
/// machine's lone hits are one hit.
@test
fn a_blow_rings_the_head_by_where_it_lands_and_no_two_blows_are_one() {
    let centre = spot_rings(0.0)
    assert(abs(centre[0] - 1.0) < 1e-5 && abs(centre[3] - 1.0) < 1e-5)
    assert(abs(centre[1]) + abs(centre[2]) + abs(centre[4]) + abs(centre[5]) < 1e-5)
    let half = spot_rings(0.5)
    assert(
        half[1] > 0.99 && half[0] < 0.75,
        f"halfway out: {half[0]} of the first, {half[1]} of the second",
    )
    var drum = drummer(SNARE_DRUM, seed: 3)
    drum.hit(1.0)
    let one = rendered(mut drum, seconds: 0.5).x
    drum.hit(1.0)
    let two = rendered(mut drum, seconds: 0.5).x
    assert(abs(one - two) > 0.002 * one, f"two lone blows of a drum: {two / one}")
    var machine = drummer(Drum { noise: 0.0, click: 0.0, ..SNARE }, seed: 3)
    machine.hit(1.0)
    let first = rendered(mut machine, seconds: 0.5).x
    machine.hit(1.0)
    let second = rendered(mut machine, seconds: 0.5).x
    assert(abs(first - second) < 1e-6 * first, f"two lone hits of the machine: {second / first}")
}
